  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 6,
    "lesson_title": "Reconstructing a Periodic Signal from Harmonics",
    "lesson_core_move": "Assemble a periodic signal by summing its DC term with sine and cosine harmonics indexed by $k$ using the fundamental angular frequency and the given trigonometric Fourier coefficients.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Reconstructing a Periodic Signal from Harmonics",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Reconstructing a Periodic Signal from Harmonics",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Reconstructing a periodic signal from harmonics means reading the coefficient list as a template for the signal, not trying to recover it from a sketch. The cue is always the same: one DC term, then cosine and sine harmonics at integer multiples of the fundamental angular frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\frac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}\\left[a_k\\cos(k\\omega_0 t)+b_k\\sin(k\\omega_0 t)\\right]",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "To build the signal, write the DC term once as $a_0/2$, then add each harmonic pair with its own index $k$: $a_k$ multiplies $\\cos(k\\omega_0 t)$ and $b_k$ multiplies $\\sin(k\\omega_0 t)$. Keep the coefficient-to-term mapping fixed, and keep the sign attached to the coefficient that produced it.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\frac{a_0}{2}+a_1\\cos(\\omega_0 t)+b_1\\sin(\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the simplest base case, the whole pattern is already visible: the DC term appears once, and the first harmonic contributes one cosine term and one sine term at $\\omega_0$. Later examples will use the same assembly rule with more values of $k$, missing terms, and coefficient tables.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Reconstructing a Periodic Signal from Harmonics",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Reconstructing a Signal from the DC Term and First Harmonic",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Reconstruct $x(t)$ when $a_0=8$, $a_1=-3$, $b_1=5$, and $\\omega_0=\\pi$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the reconstruction template from the previous section. The cue is the coefficient list, and the rule is to write the DC term once as $a_0/2$, then attach the first harmonic pair at $\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\frac{a_0}{2}+a_1\\cos(\\omega_0 t)+b_1\\sin(\\omega_0 t)=\\frac{8}{2}+(-3)\\cos(\\pi t)+5\\sin(\\pi t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Halve the DC coefficient to get $4$, keep the negative sign on $a_1=-3$, and keep $b_1=5$ on the sine term. Because $\\omega_0=\\pi$, the first harmonic appears as $\\cos(\\pi t)$ and $\\sin(\\pi t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=4-3\\cos(\\pi t)+5\\sin(\\pi t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the reconstructed signal is $x(t)=4-3\\cos(\\pi t)+5\\sin(\\pi t)$. This is the complete first-harmonic reconstruction: one DC term and one cosine-sine pair placed at the fundamental frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Reconstructing a Periodic Signal from Harmonics",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Reconstructing a Signal from Multiple Harmonics",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Reconstruct $x(t)$ when $a_0=6$, $a_1=1$, $b_1=-4$, $a_2=-2$, $b_2=3$, and $\\omega_0=2$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the reconstruction template from the previous section. The cue is the coefficient list, and the rule is to write the DC term once as $a_0/2$, then add each harmonic pair with its own index $k$ and frequency $k\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\frac{a_0}{2}+a_1\\cos(\\omega_0 t)+b_1\\sin(\\omega_0 t)+a_2\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)+b_2\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)=\\frac{6}{2}+1\\cos(2t)+(-4)\\sin(2t)+(-2)\\cos(4t)+3\\sin(4t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Write the DC term first and halve $a_0$ to get $3$. Then place the $k=1$ pair at $2t$ and the $k=2$ pair at $4t$, keeping each coefficient attached to its own harmonic index.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=3+\\cos(2t)-4\\sin(2t)-2\\cos(4t)+3\\sin(4t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the reconstructed signal is $x(t)=3+\\cos(2t)-4\\sin(2t)-2\\cos(4t)+3\\sin(4t)$. The only new step beyond the first example is adding a second harmonic pair without letting any coefficient cross to a different $k$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Reconstructing a Periodic Signal from Harmonics",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Reconstructing a Signal with Missing Harmonic Terms",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Reconstruct $x(t)$ when $a_0=10$, $a_1=-1$, $b_1=0$, $a_3=4$, $b_3=-2$, and the $k=2$ coefficients are missing.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the reconstruction template from the previous section. The cue is the coefficient list, and the rule is to write the DC term once as $a_0/2$, then keep only the harmonic terms that are actually present. Missing coefficients contribute nothing.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\frac{a_0}{2}+a_1\\cos(\\omega_0 t)+b_1\\sin(\\omega_0 t)+a_3\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)+b_3\\sin(3\\omega_0 t)=\\frac{10}{2}+(-1)\\cos(\\omega_0 t)+0\\sin(\\omega_0 t)+4\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)+(-2)\\sin(3\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Halve $a_0$ to get $5$. The $k=1$ cosine term survives as $-\\cos(\\omega_0 t)$, but the $k=1$ sine term is $0\\sin(\\omega_0 t)$, so it contributes nothing. There is no $k=2$ pair to invent, so the next surviving terms are the $k=3$ cosine and sine terms.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=5-\\cos(\\omega_0 t)+4\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)-2\\sin(3\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the reconstructed signal is $x(t)=5-\\cos(\\omega_0 t)+4\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)-2\\sin(3\\omega_0 t)$. The reusable rule is simple: write only the coefficients that are present, and treat blank or zero entries as no contribution.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Reconstructing a Periodic Signal from Harmonics",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Reconstructing a Signal from a Coefficient Table",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Reconstruct $x(t)$ from the coefficient table with $a_0=12$, $a_1=2$, $b_1=-1$, $a_2=0$, $b_2=3$, $a_3=-4$, and $b_3=0$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read the table row by row. The cue is the coefficient table, and the rule is to convert each listed $a_k$ and $b_k$ value into the matching cosine or sine term at $k\\omega_0$, while writing $a_0/2$ once at the front and ignoring zero entries.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\frac{a_0}{2}+a_1\\cos(\\omega_0 t)+b_1\\sin(\\omega_0 t)+a_2\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)+b_2\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)+a_3\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)+b_3\\sin(3\\omega_0 t)=\\frac{12}{2}+2\\cos(\\omega_0 t)+(-1)\\sin(\\omega_0 t)+0\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)+3\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)+(-4)\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)+0\\sin(3\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Halve $a_0$ to get $6$. Then read the table row by row: $a_1=2$ goes on $\\cos(\\omega_0 t)$, $b_1=-1$ goes on $\\sin(\\omega_0 t)$, $b_2=3$ goes on $\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)$, and $a_3=-4$ goes on $\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)$. The zero entries $a_2=0$ and $b_3=0$ add nothing.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=6+2\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-\\sin(\\omega_0 t)+3\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)-4\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the reconstructed signal is $x(t)=6+2\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-\\sin(\\omega_0 t)+3\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)-4\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)$. The reusable rule is to scan each row, keep the correct $k\\omega_0$ argument, and drop any zero-valued coefficient before combining the terms.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
